course: dictionary
lesson: 16125
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word whipping can mean a punishment, a heavy defeat in a game, beating cream or eggs, or moving fast like the wind. The words around it tell you which meaning is meant. Ask yourself: which meaning fits here?
explain: «She whipped the eggs and cream until they were light and fluffy.» The words eggs and cream are the clue. Whipping means beating them fast, so whipping cream is cream that has been beaten until it is thick.
explain: «Our team lost nine goals to nil. What a whipping!» In sport talk, whipping means a heavy defeat. «The wind was whipping the flags on the hill» shows movement that is fast and sudden.
explain: Whipping can also be a cruel punishment from the past, when people were hit with a whip. History books describe it, but it is now banned in most places. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: A word can have more than one meaning.
fact: «Whipping» can mean a punishment, a heavy defeat, beating cream or eggs, or moving fast like the wind.
fact: «Whipping cream» is cream that is beaten until it is thick and fluffy.
fact: A «whipping» in a game means a heavy defeat, and it is informal, so people use it in everyday talk.
fact: Whipping as a punishment was very cruel, and it is now banned in most places.
exercise: What does whipping mean in «whipping cream»? | A cruel punishment | A heavy defeat | Moving fast like the wind | *Beaten until thick and fluffy
exercise: «Our team lost nine goals to nil. What a whipping!» What does whipping mean here? | Cold, wet weather | *A heavy defeat in a game | Beating eggs fast | A cruel punishment in the past
exercise: «The wind was whipping the trees all night.» What does whipping mean? | Moving slowly and gently | Very quiet and still | A long, sad story | *Moving fast and suddenly
test: In a history book, whipping means a punishment. Which statement is true? | It was always kind and gentle | It is a soft, white cloud | *It was cruel and is now banned in most places | It is a cake people eat at parties
test: Which sentence uses whipping to mean beating eggs? | *«She was whipping the egg whites until they were fluffy.» | «The rope was whipping through the rings.» | «The flag was whipping in the breeze.»
exam: Which meaning of whipping fits best in «The wind was whipping their hats off»? | *Moving fast and suddenly | Beating cream | A heavy defeat | A cruel punishment
